<!--
  Option 4 - Foundry Local, running on your own machine.

  The model is on a laptop at http://localhost:39839, an address the gateway in
  Azure cannot route to. A Microsoft Dev Tunnel bridges the gap: the CLI on the
  laptop dials out to a Microsoft relay and publishes a stable HTTPS URL that
  forwards to the local port. To APIM it is just another backend.

  (The alternative is the APIM self-hosted gateway, which runs the data plane
  as a container on the machine so inference traffic never leaves it. That is
  the right answer for sovereign or air-gapped deployments and it is described
  in the README - it needs Docker and the Developer or Premium SKU.)

  Two things make this route work:

  1. Dev Tunnels serve an anti-phishing interstitial to browser-shaped
     requests. Without the skip header you get HTML back instead of JSON and
     the client fails with a confusing parse error. The header is set here, at
     the gateway, so no client ever has to know the tunnel exists.

  2. Timeouts are long. A laptop CPU generating tokens is the slowest backend
     in this repository by a wide margin.

  The tunnel URL is a named value so 04-foundry-local/tunnel.py can refresh it
  in one command when the tunnel is recreated.
-->
<policies>
  <inbound>
    <base />
    <include-fragment fragment-id="llm-common" />
    <set-backend-service base-url="{{foundry-local-backend-url}}" />
    <set-header name="X-Tunnel-Skip-AntiPhishing-Page" exists-action="override">
      <value>true</value>
    </set-header>
  </inbound>
  <backend>
    <forward-request timeout="600" buffer-request-body="true" />
  </backend>
  <outbound>
    <base />
  </outbound>
  <on-error>
    <base />
  </on-error>
</policies>
